message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "A Noise-Invariant Determinism Theorem for Multi-Base Post-Processing in Shor's Order Finding"
abstract: >-
  Five theorems on classical post-processing of multi-base order-finding
  measurements in Shor's algorithm: noise-invariant determinism (T1),
  logarithmic coverage time (T2), exact noise scaling 1/g_M(η) (T3),
  conditional compatibility with Regev's 2023 multi-base framework (T4),
  and a hybrid (C) + Regev b-trick factoring algorithm (T5) with closed-form
  bound matching N=437 empirical mean within 4%. Verified across 17,700
  measurements at six composite sizes. Additionally documents a universal
  trial-level mechanism observation at (N=437, d=4): across 13 base sets
  (31,200 trial-measurements), phase noise universally flips trials at one
  of the K-bin boundaries (77% K=1/K=2, 15% K=2/K=3) of hybrid factoring,
  with stochastic per-seed direction (mean SR +0.14%, p=0.31). The mechanism
  follows the classical Benzi-Buchleitner stochastic resonance shape and
  provides a conceptual bridge to the noise-as-resource literature (ENAQT).
